/// Inputs on either side of the error bound by parts of `filter_axes`, the
/// second test of the filter, with the uncertainty `e` given with each, in
/// pairs with the same `e`: the first of each pair has an exact magnitude at
/// most the bound, the sum of the `AXES_EPSILON` terms `c*d^g` and of the
/// `AXES_KAPPA` terms `c*e[k]*d^h`, the second above it, where `d_k` is the
/// largest difference of coordinates on axis `k` plus `e[k]`, as `filter_axes`
/// computes it; the two differ by one representable value in one coordinate.
///
/// The points are built as in [`bound_edge_cases`]; the scales go from just
/// above `AXES_D_MIN` to just below `AXES_D_MAX`. The first pairs have `e = 0`
/// and one scale for every axis; the others have a scale of their own on each
/// axis, one axis `2^-20` or `2^-40` times as short or as long as the other
/// two, and `e = 0`, or an `e` not zero on one axis or on every axis of the
/// range, a power of two between `2^-30` and `2^-8` times the scale of that
/// axis. In every pair with an uncertainty, the terms of kappa exceed `2^10`
/// times the terms of epsilon at both inputs, so [`check_axes_edge_cases`] can
/// require `filter_axes` to decline the first with `2 e` and to decide the
/// second with `e / 2`.
pub fn axes_edge_cases() -> Vec<([[f64; 3]; 5], [f64; 3])> {
    AXES_EDGE_CASES
        .into_iter()
        .map(|(case, e)| {
            (
                case.map(|point| point.map(f64::from_bits)),
                e.map(f64::from_bits),
            )
        })
        .collect()
}

